# Bound controls

`:select`, `:radio` and `:checkbox` mean two things, and where they sit decides which. Inside a `:form` they are form fields, submitted by name. Outside one they bind to a declared `:state` the way `:bind` and `:slider` already do: move the control and the state changes, change the state and the control moves.

</section>

<section>

## A settings panel

:state density = "Comfortable"
:state theme_pick = "System"
:state previews = true

**Density**

:select density
- Compact
- Comfortable
- Spacious

**Colour**

:radio theme_pick
- System
- Light
- Dark

**Previews**

:checkbox previews
- Show image previews

</section>

<section>

## What the panel drives

Every region below reads the same state the controls write.

:if density == "Compact"
Compact: rows sit tight together.
:else if density == "Spacious"
Spacious: rows get room to breathe.
:else
Comfortable: the default spacing.
:endif

:if theme_pick == "System"
Following the operating system's colour scheme.
:else
Forcing **{ theme_pick }** regardless of the system setting.
:endif

:if previews
Image previews are on.
:else
Image previews are off, so the list loads text only.
:endif

Settings as one line each: **{ density }**, **{ theme_pick }**, previews { previews }.

:button "Reset to defaults" -> density reset; theme_pick reset; previews reset

</section>

<section>

## The rules

- The state has to exist first. A bound field with no matching `:state` is a compile error that names both readings, so a typo can never become a silently inert control.
- A bound `:radio` group carries the chosen option's text; the group keeps a shared `name` so the browser makes it mutually exclusive.
- A bound `:checkbox` carries a **boolean**, so it takes exactly one `- Label` line — the label to show beside it. The multi-value checkbox group is the in-form behavior and is unchanged.
- Inside a `:form` nothing changed at all, even if a `:state` of the same name happens to exist elsewhere on the page.

</section>

